  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 125 AM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nThrough Saturday night: Dry and cooler conditions will prevail in\nthe wake of a cold front that pushed across the area earlier this\nweek. Substantially colder and drier air is settling into the \narea with temperatures this morning some 10-15 F cooler than 24\nhours ago. Overnight lows continue downward tonight into Saturday\nnight when a freeze is increasingly likely across much of north \nand east MS with a frost possible over a broader area. A Freeze \nWatch issued on Thursday for that area has been upgraded to a \nFreeze Warning valid for both Friday night and Saturday night when\nthe coldest locations will could see temperatures into the upper\n20s F for the first time this Fall season.\n\nBecause few areas have received wetting rains recently, the \ncombination of low humidity and breezy conditions in the wake of \nthe front resulted in in elevated fire concerns Thursday and we'll\nsee a similar situation today with even lower RH anticipated \nparticularly during this afternoon. Lower daytime temperatures \nmay help mitigate the threat somewhat, but given the ongoing \ndrought/dry fuels and continued breezy conditions, fire danger \nwill persist. /86/DL/ \n\nSunday through next week: In the wake of the longwave upper \ntrough currently over the eastern CONUS, a secondary shortwave \nwill follow Sunday into Monday, with a surface system tracking \nfrom Texas into the northern Gulf. Scattered showers on the \nnorthern periphery of this system may spread into southern \nportions of our area late Sunday into Monday, though there are \nsome model solutions showing little to no rain making it into our \narea. Regardless, any rainfall with this system would be light. \nOtherwise, surface ridging will build back in through mid-week \nwith dry weather and gradually moderating temperatures. /DL/",
